Findings: [
  "The sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called **scattering** of sunlight by the Earth's atmosphere.\n\nHere is a simple breakdown:\n\n1. **Sunlight is White:** Sunlight looks white, but it is actually made up of all the colors of the rainbow (red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet).\n\n2. **Atmospheric Particles:** The Earth's atmosphere is made up of tiny molecules, primarily nitrogen and oxygen.\n\n3. **Rayleigh Scattering:** When sunlight enters the atmosphere, it hits these tiny molecules. This causes the light to scatter (or get bounced) in all directions. This specific type of scattering, called **Rayleigh scattering**, is very effective at scattering shorter wavelengths of light more than longer ones.\n\n4. **Blue is Scattered Most:** Blue and violet light have shorter wavelengths than red and orange light. Therefore, the atmosphere scatters the **blue light** much more effectively across the entire sky than the other colors.\n\n**In simple terms:** The blue light is scattered all over the sky, making the sky look blue to us when we look up."
]
